From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: offload TCP using the
 parser's control-flag exceptions

No TCP flow has ever been hardware-offloaded by this driver.
nf_flow_rule_match() builds every TCP flow with tcp.flags = 0 masked to
FIN|RST -- it is asking the driver to accelerate only segments with those
flags clear, so conntrack still observes the connection close. A PPE flow
entry matches on the 5-tuple alone and cannot express that, so
ppe_offload_flow_replace() declined any flow carrying the mask. Since the
mask is present on *every* TCP flow, that declined all of them, leaving
only UDP accelerated. It was invisible because the decline is pr_debug and
this target builds with CONFIG_DYNAMIC_DEBUG unset.

Simply accepting the flow is not an option. NF_FLOW_CLOSING is only ever
set on the software forwarding path (nf_flow_table_ip.c), so an offloaded
flow never sets it and flow_offload_fixup_ct() falls through to
timeouts[ESTABLISHED] instead of timeouts[CLOSE] -- 7440s rather than 10s
on a stock OpenWrt. Every closed-but-offloaded connection would sit in
conntrack for two hours; at nf_conntrack_max = 55808 a sustained 7.5
offloaded connections/second exhausts the table.

Hardware has the right mechanism, and the vendor driver uses it. The
ingress parser holds eight TCP flag value/mask slots, each raising its own
exception; L3_EXCEPTION_CMD gives an exception its action and
L3_EXP_L3_FLOW_CTRL enables it for packets that hit an L3 flow entry.
qca-nss-ppe programs FIN, SYN and RST as redirect-to-CPU with
de-acceleration, and mtk_ppe / airoha_ppe do the equivalent with their own
TCP-FIN ageing. Program the same three here and accept TCP flows whose
mask asks for nothing outside them.

The registers are taken from the APPE branch of the vendor headers, as
IPQ5332 is an APPE/MPPE part (see patch 0434):

  L4_EXCEPTION_PARSING_CTRL_0..3  IPR_CSR  0x1e0000 + 0x28/0x2c/0x30/0x34
  L3_EXCEPTION_CMD                IPE_L3   0x200000 + 0x81c, 72 entries
  L3_FLOW_HIT_EXP_CTRL            IPE_L3   0x200000 + 0x1678, 72 entries

Note the enable is L3_FLOW_HIT_EXP_CTRL, not the L3_EXP_L3_FLOW_CTRL that
the register name suggests: on APPE the SDK splits the generic "any L3
flow" enable into hit and miss registers and clears the generic one
whenever it uses them. Scoping to a flow hit also confines this change to
traffic the driver already offloads -- a FIN on a connection nobody
offloaded raises no exception and takes the ordinary software path.

Because picking the wrong branch is silent -- which is exactly what 0434
had to undo -- the init path reads a slot back and refuses to offload TCP
if it does not hold what was just written. That failure is not fatal: UDP
offload does not depend on these exceptions, so TCP simply keeps being
declined rather than being accelerated unsafely.

That guard earned itself on the first hardware boot: ppe_offload_init()
runs before ppe_clock_init_and_reset() and ppe_hw_config(), so register
writes from it land on a block that is still unclocked and in reset. The
readback returned 0x3 instead of the 0x0101 written. Hardware programming
therefore moves to a separate ppe_offload_hw_init() called after
ppe_hw_config(); until it runs tcp_exceptions_ok is false, and no traffic
can reach the offload path before edma_setup() brings the netdevs up.

Measured on a GL-BE9300 with a 1 Gbit LAN client: a TCP flow reaches
[HW_OFFLOAD] and runs at 937 Mbit/s with the CPU 96% idle; on close it
enters CLOSE with the 10s timeout and is gone in ~12s instead of being
held as ESTABLISHED; and 80 short connections in a row leave no residue
(conntrack settles to 20 entries from a 96 baseline, with none of the
test's own 5-tuples left).

The readback covers every field on every slot -- parser value and mask,
the action word, and both the flow-hit and flow-miss enables -- because
checking one is not enough: the whole parser window is writable, so a
wrong-branch address would read back whatever was written to it and pass.
The action word matters most: a slot that raises its exception while the
action sits at its reset value (FORWARD) would accelerate the teardown
anyway, reintroducing the bug while looking fully configured.

Also make the decline diagnostic pr_info_once per call site rather than
ratelimited, so each distinct reason announces itself once per boot and is
then silent. Declining is the normal case here -- the reply direction of
every NAT connection and every IPv6 flow -- and flow_offload_refresh()
retries roughly once a second per live flow, so a ratelimited print would
sit at its ceiling permanently. Gate TCP on the protocol rather than on
the presence of the TCP dissector key, so a hand-written tc filter cannot
reach hardware with the exceptions unprogrammed. Document the shared
ingress-L3-interface limitation (the flow key cannot distinguish two
firewall zones sharing an EDMA conduit) and record that the exceptions
become the sole teardown mechanism once the reply direction is offloaded.
Finally correct the ppe_flow_ipv4.h comment on @deacclr_en, which
described it as a conditional TCP-flag mechanism. It is not: it applies to
every packet matching the entry and its action is the global
L3_ROUTE_CTRL.FLOW_DE_ACCE_CMD, which resets to redirect-to-CPU.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe.c
@@ -252,6 +252,11 @@ static int qcom_ppe_probe(struct platfor
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "PPE HW config failed\n");
 	}
 
+	/* Needs the PPE clocked and out of reset, so it cannot live in
+	 * ppe_offload_init() above.
+	 */
+	ppe_offload_hw_init(ppe_dev);
+
 	ret = edma_setup(ppe_dev);
 	if (ret) {
 		ppe_offload_destroy(ppe_dev);
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_regs.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_regs.h
@@ -1392,4 +1392,83 @@
 
 /* Word 5. */
 #define PPE_FLOW_TBL_PMTU_1			GENMASK(2, 0)
+
+/* TCP-flag exception matchers, in the ingress parser block ("Ingress parse",
+ * 0x1e0000, in ppe_readable_ranges[]). Hardware compares each TCP segment's
+ * flags against eight independent value/mask slots; a hit raises its own
+ * exception, which L3_EXCEPTION_CMD below turns into an action. Eight slots
+ * are packed two per register across four consecutive registers.
+ *
+ * Vendor: IPR_CSR_BASE_ADDR (0x1e0000, the APPE branch -- see patch 0434) +
+ * L4_EXCEPTION_PARSING_CTRL_{0,1,2,3}_REG_ADDRESS (0x28, 0x2c, 0x30, 0x34).
+ */
+#define PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_PARSING_CTRL_ADDR	0x1e0028
+#define PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_PARSING_CTRL_INC	0x4
+#define PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAG_SLOTS		8
+
+/* Even slot in the low half of its register, odd slot in the high half. */
+#define PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_EVEN		GENMASK(5, 0)
+#define PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_EVEN_MASK	GENMASK(13, 8)
+#define PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_ODD		GENMASK(21, 16)
+#define PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_ODD_MASK	GENMASK(29, 24)
+
+/* The six TCP control flags hardware matches on, in their on-the-wire order. */
+#define PPE_TCP_FLAG_FIN			BIT(0)
+#define PPE_TCP_FLAG_SYN			BIT(1)
+#define PPE_TCP_FLAG_RST			BIT(2)
+#define PPE_TCP_FLAG_PSH			BIT(3)
+#define PPE_TCP_FLAG_ACK			BIT(4)
+#define PPE_TCP_FLAG_URG			BIT(5)
+
+/* Per-exception action, and per-exception enable for packets that hit an L3
+ * flow entry. Both are 72-entry arrays indexed by exception code, in IPE_L3.
+ *
+ * Vendor: IPE_L3_BASE_ADDR (0x200000) + L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_ADDRESS (0x81c) and
+ * + L3_EXP_L3_FLOW_CTRL_ADDRESS (0xcdc), both the APPE branch.
+ */
+#define PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_ADDR		0x20081c
+#define PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_INC		0x4
+#define PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_ENTRIES		72
+#define PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_CMD		GENMASK(1, 0)
+#define PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_DE_ACCE		BIT(2)
+
+#define PPE_L3_EXP_L3_FLOW_CTRL_ADDR		0x200cdc
+#define PPE_L3_EXP_L3_FLOW_CTRL_INC		0x4
+#define PPE_L3_EXP_L3_FLOW_CTRL_EXCEP_EN	BIT(0)
+
+/* On APPE the "any L3 flow" enable above is superseded by a pair that splits
+ * flow hit from flow miss, and the SDK sets the generic one to 0 whenever it
+ * uses them (adpt_hppe_sec_l3_excep_ctrl_set(), "#if defined(APPE)" branch).
+ * Only the hit register is wanted here: an exception must fire for a packet
+ * that matched an offloaded flow, never for one that missed.
+ *
+ * Vendor: IPE_L3_BASE_ADDR (0x200000) + L3_FLOW_HIT_EXP_CTRL_ADDRESS (0x1678).
+ */
+#define PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_EXP_CTRL_ADDR		0x201678
+#define PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_EXP_CTRL_INC		0x4
+#define PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_EXP_CTRL_EXCEP_EN	BIT(0)
+
+/* The flow-MISS half of that APPE split. Wanted off: an exception must fire for
+ * a packet that matched an offloaded flow, never for one that missed -- a miss
+ * goes to the CPU by the ordinary path anyway. Its reset default is documented
+ * as 0, but this driver programs the shared action register for the same
+ * exception codes, so leave nothing to assumption and clear it explicitly.
+ *
+ * Vendor: IPE_L3_BASE_ADDR (0x200000) + L3_FLOW_HIT_MISS_EXP_CTRL_ADDRESS (0x17a8).
+ */
+#define PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_MISS_EXP_CTRL_ADDR	0x2017a8
+#define PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_MISS_EXP_CTRL_INC	0x4
+#define PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_MISS_EXP_CTRL_EXCEP_EN	BIT(0)
+
+/* Action values for PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_CMD (vendor fal_fwd_cmd_t). */
+#define PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_FORWARD		0
+#define PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_DROP			1
+#define PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_COPY_TO_CPU		2
+#define PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_REDIRECT_TO_CPU	3
+
+/* Exception code of the first TCP-flag slot (vendor FAL_SEC_EXP_TCP_FLAGS_0);
+ * slot n is at PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_0 + n.
+ */
+#define PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_0		46
+
 #endif
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_offload.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_offload.h
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@ struct net_device;
 enum tc_setup_type;
 
 int ppe_offload_init(struct ppe_device *ppe_dev);
+void ppe_offload_hw_init(struct ppe_device *ppe_dev);
 void ppe_offload_destroy(struct ppe_device *ppe_dev);
 
 int ppe_offload_setup_tc(struct net_device *netdev, enum tc_setup_type type,
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_offload.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_offload.c
@@ -51,6 +51,36 @@
  *   which is what stops the flowtable ageing out a connection that
  *   hardware is still forwarding.
  *
+ * - TCP is accepted only because the parser's control-flag exceptions
+ *   (FIN/SYN/RST -> redirect to CPU + de-accelerate, armed on an L3
+ *   flow hit) are programmed and read back at init; see
+ *   ppe_offload_tcp_exception_init(). Without them the flowtable's
+ *   FIN|RST mask cannot be honoured and a teardown would be
+ *   accelerated, leaving conntrack holding closed connections at
+ *   timeouts[ESTABLISHED] instead of timeouts[CLOSE].
+ *
+ *   NOTE for whoever implements reply-direction offload: today a
+ *   remote peer's FIN/RST always traverses the software path anyway,
+ *   because the reply direction is declined, and that is what sets
+ *   NF_FLOW_CLOSING. The exceptions are therefore currently a second
+ *   line of defence for the LAN-initiated case (an RST abort draws no
+ *   reply). Once the reply direction IS offloaded they become the only
+ *   mechanism -- do not weaken them in that milestone.
+ *
+ * - SECURITY LIMITATION: a flow entry is keyed on the 5-tuple plus an
+ *   ingress L3 interface, and nothing finer. ppe_offload_ingress_edma_dev()
+ *   resolves a VLAN upper and a DSA user port down to the conduit, so
+ *   every DSA port and every VLAN on that conduit collapse into one
+ *   src_l3_if -- whereas the software flowtable key it stands in for
+ *   carries the exact iifidx. Where two firewall zones share a conduit
+ *   (this board runs a trusted and a guest VLAN over the same one), a
+ *   host in the weaker zone can source-spoof a victim's offloaded
+ *   5-tuple and have silicon forward and translate its packets with no
+ *   software inspection -- no rp_filter, no interface-scoped rule. The
+ *   hardware offers no finer qualifier, so this is a property of
+ *   enabling the flowtable across such a boundary, not something this
+ *   driver can validate away.
+ *
  * Hardware itself may also not consult the flow table at all yet for
  * genuinely external ingress traffic -- ppe_servcode_init() bypasses
  * flow lookup (PPE_SC_BYPASS_INGRESS_FLOW_LOOKUP) only for service
@@ -72,6 +102,7 @@
 #include <linux/list.h>
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/netdevice.h>
+#include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <net/dsa.h>
 #include <linux/rhashtable.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
@@ -152,6 +183,11 @@ static const struct rhashtable_params pp
  * and ppe_offload_flow_replace()/_destroy() for how it's used to stop
  * a superseded entry's later destroy from deleting a different, live
  * flow's hardware entry.
+ * @tcp_exceptions_ok: Whether the TCP control-flag exceptions were programmed
+ * and read back correctly at init. TCP flows are only offloaded when they
+ * were: without them hardware would accelerate a FIN/RST teardown that the
+ * flowtable explicitly asked us not to -- see
+ * ppe_offload_tcp_exception_init().
  * @lock: Serializes the whole replace/destroy sequence -- looking up
  * @flow_table, allocating from the pools, and programming hardware --
  * against concurrent calls for different flows, mirroring
@@ -164,6 +200,7 @@ struct ppe_offload_priv {
 	struct ppe_pub_ip_pool pub_ip_pool;
 	struct rhashtable flow_table;
 	struct ppe_offload_flow_entry **flow_owner;
+	bool tcp_exceptions_ok;
 	struct mutex lock;
 };
 
@@ -239,12 +276,27 @@ static int ppe_offload_apply_l4_mangle(s
 	return 0;
 }
 
-/* Diagnostic: logs exactly which check declined a flow. pr_debug so an
- * unauthenticated client cannot flood the log by generating unoffloadable
- * flows; enable with dynamic debug when investigating a silent decline.
+/* Diagnostic: logs exactly which check declined a flow, once per reason.
+ *
+ * This target builds with CONFIG_DYNAMIC_DEBUG unset, so a plain pr_debug
+ * compiles to nothing and leaves "offload silently does not happen"
+ * indistinguishable from "offload is not enabled" -- that cost a full
+ * hardware-test cycle to diagnose. But a ratelimited print is far too loud
+ * here: declining is the NORMAL case on this board (the reply direction of
+ * every NAT connection is declined, as is every IPv6 flow), and
+ * flow_offload_refresh() retries an unoffloaded flow about once a second for
+ * as long as it lives. A handful of ordinary connections would sit at the
+ * ratelimit ceiling forever and push everything else out of the ring buffer.
+ *
+ * pr_info_once expands a separate static flag per call site, so each distinct
+ * reason announces itself exactly once per boot and is then silent -- the
+ * diagnostic signal without the steady-state noise. The pr_debug keeps the
+ * per-occurrence detail available to anyone who does build with dynamic debug.
  */
 #define ppe_offload_decline(fmt, ...) \
-	({ pr_debug("ppe_offload: declined - " fmt "\n", ##__VA_ARGS__); -EOPNOTSUPP; })
+	({ pr_info_once("ppe_offload: declined - " fmt " (further declines for this reason are silent)\n", \
+			 ##__VA_ARGS__); \
+	   pr_debug("ppe_offload: declined - " fmt "\n", ##__VA_ARGS__); -EOPNOTSUPP; })
 
 /* Resolve the device a flow's packets arrive on down to the EDMA port
  * whose ingress L3 interface hardware will match against, mirroring
@@ -341,6 +393,15 @@ static int ppe_offload_flow_replace(stru
 		flow_rule_match_basic(rule, &basic);
 		switch (basic.key->ip_proto) {
 		case IPPROTO_TCP:
+			/* Fail closed. Keyed on the protocol, not on the presence of
+			 * FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_TCP or a non-zero flag mask: the
+			 * flowtable always supplies both, but a hand-written tc
+			 * flower filter on this same block need not, and such a rule
+			 * must not slip past into hardware while the control-flag
+			 * exceptions are unprogrammed.
+			 */
+			if (!priv->tcp_exceptions_ok)
+				return ppe_offload_decline("tcp offload disabled: control-flag exceptions unavailable");
 			ip_proto = PPE_FLOW_PROTO_TCP;
 			break;
 		case IPPROTO_UDP:
@@ -371,17 +432,39 @@ static int ppe_offload_flow_replace(stru
 	 */
 	/* nf_flow_rule_match() builds TCP flows with tcp.flags = 0 masked to
 	 * RST|FIN, precisely so that a teardown is NOT accelerated and conntrack
-	 * still observes it. We program no TCP-flag exception, so an entry would
-	 * match a superset of the software rule and swallow FIN/RST. Decline
-	 * instead of silently accelerating more than we were asked to.
+	 * still observes it. A flow entry matches on the 5-tuple alone, so that
+	 * condition is carried by the parser-level TCP-flag exceptions programmed
+	 * in ppe_offload_tcp_exception_init(): a FIN, SYN or RST segment is
+	 * redirected to the CPU and de-accelerates the flow.
+	 *
+	 * netfilter expresses the mask in the same bit order hardware uses (the
+	 * low byte of the TCP flags word), so the vendor PPE_TCP_FLAG_* values
+	 * are directly comparable against it.
 	 */
 	if (flow_rule_match_key(rule, FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_TCP)) {
+		u16 covered = PPE_TCP_FLAG_FIN | PPE_TCP_FLAG_SYN | PPE_TCP_FLAG_RST;
 		struct flow_match_tcp tcp;
+		u16 flag_mask, flag_key;
 
 		flow_rule_match_tcp(rule, &tcp);
-		if (tcp.mask->flags)
+		flag_mask = be16_to_cpu(tcp.mask->flags);
+		flag_key = be16_to_cpu(tcp.key->flags);
+
+		/* Anything outside the flags we punt to the CPU would be a
+		 * condition the hardware entry silently ignores.
+		 */
+		if (flag_mask & ~covered)
 			return ppe_offload_decline("tcp flag mask %#x not honoured",
-						    be16_to_cpu(tcp.mask->flags));
+						    flag_mask);
+
+		/* The exceptions can only EXCLUDE a flagged segment from
+		 * acceleration. A rule wanting to match packets that HAVE one
+		 * of these flags set is the opposite, and unimplementable here.
+		 */
+		if (flag_key & flag_mask)
+			return ppe_offload_decline("tcp flag key %#x/%#x not honoured",
+						    flag_key, flag_mask);
+
 	}
 
 	if (!flow_rule_match_key(rule, FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_META))
@@ -1018,6 +1101,244 @@ static int ppe_offload_indr_setup_tc_cb(
 	}
 }
 
+/* TCP control flags that must never be accelerated.
+ *
+ * nf_flow_rule_match() builds every TCP flow with tcp.flags = 0 masked to
+ * FIN|RST: the flowtable is asking us to accelerate only segments with those
+ * flags clear, so that conntrack still sees the connection close. A PPE flow
+ * entry matches on the 5-tuple alone and cannot express a flag condition, so
+ * the parser-level exceptions programmed below carry it instead -- a segment
+ * carrying one of these flags is redirected to the CPU and de-accelerates its
+ * flow, leaving the software path to finish the teardown.
+ *
+ * SYN is included, beyond what the mask asks for, because it is how a new
+ * connection reusing a still-live 5-tuple announces itself; the software fast
+ * path makes the same check (nf_flow_table_ip.c keys TCP reopen off tcph->syn).
+ * Without it hardware would forward the new connection under the old entry's
+ * translation. The vendor driver programs exactly these three.
+ */
+static const u8 ppe_offload_tcp_exception_flags[] = {
+	PPE_TCP_FLAG_FIN,
+	PPE_TCP_FLAG_SYN,
+	PPE_TCP_FLAG_RST,
+};
+
+/* Point one parser slot at @flags. Value and mask are set to the same bits, so
+ * hardware hits when those flags are set and ignores the others -- FIN,ACK, the
+ * ordinary way a peer closes, still matches the FIN slot.
+ */
+static int ppe_offload_tcp_exception_slot(struct ppe_device *ppe_dev,
+					   unsigned int slot, u8 flags)
+{
+	u32 addr, mask, val;
+
+	addr = PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_PARSING_CTRL_ADDR +
+		(slot / 2) * PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_PARSING_CTRL_INC;
+
+	if (slot % 2) {
+		mask = PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_ODD |
+			PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_ODD_MASK;
+		val = FIELD_PREP(PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_ODD, flags) |
+			FIELD_PREP(PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_ODD_MASK, flags);
+	} else {
+		mask = PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_EVEN |
+			PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_EVEN_MASK;
+		val = FIELD_PREP(PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_EVEN, flags) |
+			FIELD_PREP(PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_EVEN_MASK, flags);
+	}
+
+	return regmap_update_bits(ppe_dev->regmap, addr, mask, val);
+}
+
+/* Give a TCP-flag slot's exception its action: redirect the segment to the CPU
+ * and de-accelerate the flow, and enable the exception for packets that hit an
+ * L3 flow entry (without that, an accelerated flow would bypass it entirely).
+ *
+ * Scoping it to a flow *hit* is what keeps this change confined to traffic the
+ * driver already offloads -- a FIN on a connection nobody offloaded never
+ * raises the exception and takes the ordinary software path.
+ */
+static int ppe_offload_tcp_exception_action(struct ppe_device *ppe_dev,
+					     unsigned int slot)
+{
+	u32 index = PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_0 + slot;
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = regmap_update_bits(ppe_dev->regmap,
+				  PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_ADDR +
+				  index * PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_INC,
+				  PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_CMD | PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_DE_ACCE,
+				  FIELD_PREP(PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_CMD,
+					      PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_REDIRECT_TO_CPU) |
+				  PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_DE_ACCE);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/* APPE splits the generic "any L3 flow" enable into hit and miss, and
+	 * the generic one must be left clear when they are used.
+	 */
+	ret = regmap_update_bits(ppe_dev->regmap,
+				  PPE_L3_EXP_L3_FLOW_CTRL_ADDR +
+				  index * PPE_L3_EXP_L3_FLOW_CTRL_INC,
+				  PPE_L3_EXP_L3_FLOW_CTRL_EXCEP_EN, 0);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = regmap_update_bits(ppe_dev->regmap,
+				  PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_EXP_CTRL_ADDR +
+				  index * PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_EXP_CTRL_INC,
+				  PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_EXP_CTRL_EXCEP_EN,
+				  PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_EXP_CTRL_EXCEP_EN);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/* And the miss half explicitly off. Its reset default is 0, but this
+	 * function has just repointed the shared action for these exception
+	 * codes at redirect-to-CPU, so a stale enable here would start punting
+	 * every TCP control segment that missed the flow table too.
+	 */
+	return regmap_update_bits(ppe_dev->regmap,
+				   PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_MISS_EXP_CTRL_ADDR +
+				   index * PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_MISS_EXP_CTRL_INC,
+				   PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_MISS_EXP_CTRL_EXCEP_EN, 0);
+}
+
+/**
+ * ppe_offload_tcp_exception_init() - Program the TCP control-flag exceptions.
+ * @ppe_dev: PPE device.
+ *
+ * Return: true if TCP flows may be offloaded, false if they must keep being
+ * declined.
+ *
+ * Failure is deliberately not fatal. UDP offload does not depend on these
+ * exceptions, so a failure here costs TCP acceleration and nothing else --
+ * whereas offloading TCP without them would silently swallow FIN/RST and leave
+ * conntrack holding closed connections as ESTABLISHED.
+ */
+static bool ppe_offload_tcp_exception_init(struct ppe_device *ppe_dev)
+{
+	unsigned int i;
+	u32 val;
+	int ret;
+
+	BUILD_BUG_ON(ARRAY_SIZE(ppe_offload_tcp_exception_flags) >
+		      PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAG_SLOTS);
+	BUILD_BUG_ON(PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_0 + PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAG_SLOTS >
+		      PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_ENTRIES);
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(ppe_offload_tcp_exception_flags); i++) {
+		ret = ppe_offload_tcp_exception_slot(ppe_dev, i,
+						      ppe_offload_tcp_exception_flags[i]);
+		if (!ret)
+			ret = ppe_offload_tcp_exception_action(ppe_dev, i);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(ppe_dev->dev,
+				"failed to program TCP exception slot %u: %d\n", i, ret);
+			return false;
+		}
+	}
+
+	/* Verify EVERY field that was just written, on every slot, before
+	 * trusting any of it.
+	 *
+	 * The point is not to re-check the address arithmetic -- that is
+	 * readable in the code above -- but to catch silicon interpreting the
+	 * register map differently from the vendor headers, which is exactly
+	 * the mistake patch 0434 had to undo and is completely silent. Checking
+	 * one slot is not enough: the whole 0x1e0000 parser window is writable,
+	 * so a wrong-branch address would read back whatever was written to it
+	 * and pass. The safety-critical one is the action register: if a slot
+	 * raised its exception but the action stayed at its reset value
+	 * (FORWARD), the segment would be accelerated anyway and TCP teardown
+	 * would be silently swallowed -- the 7440s-ESTABLISHED bug this whole
+	 * patch exists to prevent, with every indication of being configured.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(ppe_offload_tcp_exception_flags); i++) {
+		u8 want = ppe_offload_tcp_exception_flags[i];
+		u32 index = PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_0 + i;
+		u32 got_val, got_mask;
+
+		ret = regmap_read(ppe_dev->regmap,
+				   PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_PARSING_CTRL_ADDR +
+				   (i / 2) * PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_PARSING_CTRL_INC, &val);
+		if (ret)
+			goto read_failed;
+
+		if (i % 2) {
+			got_val = FIELD_GET(PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_ODD, val);
+			got_mask = FIELD_GET(PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_ODD_MASK, val);
+		} else {
+			got_val = FIELD_GET(PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_EVEN, val);
+			got_mask = FIELD_GET(PPE_L4_EXCEPTION_TCP_FLAGS_EVEN_MASK, val);
+		}
+
+		if (got_val != want || got_mask != want) {
+			dev_err(ppe_dev->dev,
+				"TCP exception slot %u parser mismatch: flags %#x/%#x, wanted %#x/%#x\n",
+				i, got_val, got_mask, want, want);
+			return false;
+		}
+
+		ret = regmap_read(ppe_dev->regmap,
+				   PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_ADDR +
+				   index * PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_INC, &val);
+		if (ret)
+			goto read_failed;
+
+		if (FIELD_GET(PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_CMD, val) != PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_REDIRECT_TO_CPU ||
+		    !(val & PPE_L3_EXCEPTION_CMD_DE_ACCE)) {
+			dev_err(ppe_dev->dev,
+				"TCP exception %u action mismatch (%#x); would not punt the segment\n",
+				index, val);
+			return false;
+		}
+
+		ret = regmap_read(ppe_dev->regmap,
+				   PPE_L3_EXP_L3_FLOW_CTRL_ADDR +
+				   index * PPE_L3_EXP_L3_FLOW_CTRL_INC, &val);
+		if (ret)
+			goto read_failed;
+
+		if (val & PPE_L3_EXP_L3_FLOW_CTRL_EXCEP_EN) {
+			dev_err(ppe_dev->dev,
+				"TCP exception %u: generic flow enable still set (%#x)\n",
+				index, val);
+			return false;
+		}
+
+		ret = regmap_read(ppe_dev->regmap,
+				   PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_EXP_CTRL_ADDR +
+				   index * PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_EXP_CTRL_INC, &val);
+		if (ret)
+			goto read_failed;
+
+		if (!(val & PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_EXP_CTRL_EXCEP_EN)) {
+			dev_err(ppe_dev->dev,
+				"TCP exception %u not armed on flow hit (%#x)\n", index, val);
+			return false;
+		}
+
+		ret = regmap_read(ppe_dev->regmap,
+				   PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_MISS_EXP_CTRL_ADDR +
+				   index * PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_MISS_EXP_CTRL_INC, &val);
+		if (ret)
+			goto read_failed;
+
+		if (val & PPE_L3_FLOW_HIT_MISS_EXP_CTRL_EXCEP_EN) {
+			dev_err(ppe_dev->dev,
+				"TCP exception %u still armed on flow miss (%#x)\n", index, val);
+			return false;
+		}
+	}
+
+	dev_dbg(ppe_dev->dev, "TCP control-flag exceptions programmed and verified\n");
+	return true;
+
+read_failed:
+	dev_err(ppe_dev->dev, "TCP exception readback failed: %d\n", ret);
+	return false;
+}
